    val INET_CMD_GET_STATS           = 50
    val INET_CMD_FRAME_POOL_STATS    = 51
    val INET_CMD_TCP_OOO_SELFTEST = 99


Sub-ops for INET_CMD_ARP_PROBE. A single command code fans out
into three operations so RFC 5227 conflict detection can be
driven from userspace:

- `START` arms the probe state with a target IP.
- `SEND` transmits one ARP probe (sender IP = 0.0.0.0).
- `CHECK` returns whether any defending ARP was observed and
  clears the probe state.

    val ARP_PROBE_OP_START    = 0
    val ARP_PROBE_OP_SEND     = 1
    val ARP_PROBE_OP_CHECK    = 2
    val ARP_PROBE_OP_ANNOUNCE = 3


Capacity limits.

    val INET_MAX_SOCKETS = 32
    val INET_MAX_QUEUE   = 2
    val INET_MAX_DATA    = 1472
    val INET_MSG_BUF     = 1500  // cmd(1)+fd(1)+hdr(9)+maxlen(2)+data(1472)+slop


Frame buffer pool (Phase 3 chunk 1) — storage + helpers live in
`inet_proto.lsysl` (cross-file VAR access fails the sysl analyzer
per `feedback_sysl_cross_unit.md`, so the pool follows the same
"every helper sits with the var it touches" rule used by
`inet_count_*_owner`). Forward calls from this file into
`inet_frame_alloc` / `inet_frame_release` / `inet_frame_buf`
work fine — sysl resolves them on the second pass.


A queued datagram awaiting recvfrom. Source address is stored
host-order to simplify comparisons. Sized to the full Ethernet
MTU (1500 - 20 IP - 8 UDP = 1472) so DNS/EDNS(0), QUIC, and
real-world UDP traffic fit without truncation.

    struct InetDgram
        src_ip: u32
        src_port: int
        data: [1472]byte
        data_len: int


Per-socket state. `bound_port == 0` means unbound (port 0 is
reserved for auto-bind, which isn't implemented yet). Queue
depth dropped from 4 → 2 when InetDgram payload jumped from 512
→ 1472 bytes; keeps the inet server binary inside the rs module
buffer cap. Two slots is plenty of burst absorption for normal
traffic.

    struct InetSocket
        in_use: int
        owner_tid: int
        bound: int
        bound_ip: u32
        bound_port: int
        queue: [2]InetDgram
        q_head: int
        q_tail: int
        q_count: int
        pending_tid: int
        pending_maxlen: int
        recv_deadline: int
        pending_error: int
        // Phase 3 chunk 2: forward link in the UDP-port hash bucket
        // for `bound_port`. -1 = not in any bucket. Maintained by
        // `inet_udp_hash_insert` / `inet_udp_hash_remove` whenever a
        // socket transitions in/out of the bound state.
        next_in_udp_bucket: int

    var inet_sockets: [32]InetSocket
    var inet_port = -1


### UDP port hash (Phase 3 chunk 2)

`inet_find_bound` and `inet_find_bound_port` are the two hot lookups
on the inbound UDP path: every received datagram pays one of them to
find the destination socket. A linear scan over the 32-slot table is
fine for current loads, but the plan calls for hashing now while the
caps are small so growth doesn't surface a perf wall.

The hash is a chained table keyed on `bound_port & (N - 1)`. N is a
power of two so the mask is a single `&`. Each socket holds a
`next_in_udp_bucket` link forming a singly-linked list per bucket;
`inet_udp_port_hash[h]` is the head index, or -1 for empty. With
N=16 and 32 sockets we average 2 entries per bucket — already a 16x
constant-factor win over the linear scan, and it scales linearly
with N if MAX_SOCKETS grows.

Maintenance points: `inet_handle_bind` and the auto-bind in
`inet_handle_sendto` insert; `inet_handle_close` and
`inet_handle_pid_exit` remove. Tests catch missed maintenance via
the duplicate-bind detection in `inet_handle_bind` (collisions or
stale entries surface as either a refused legitimate bind or an
allowed dup bind).

    val INET_UDP_HASH_N = 16

    var inet_udp_port_hash: [16]int

Hash a port to a bucket. Limited to the low 16 bits per the IPv4
TCP/UDP port-number space.

    inet_udp_bucket(port: int) -> int
        (port & 0xffff) & (INET_UDP_HASH_N - 1)

Initialize the bucket array. Called once from `inet_init_sockets`.

    inet_udp_hash_init()
        var i = 0
        while i < INET_UDP_HASH_N
            inet_udp_port_hash[i] = -1
            i += 1

Insert socket `idx` at the head of its `bound_port` bucket. Caller
guarantees `bound_port` is set and the socket isn't already in any
bucket (i.e. only call after a fresh bind).

    inet_udp_hash_insert(idx: int)
        val h = inet_udp_bucket(inet_sockets[idx].bound_port)
        inet_sockets[idx].next_in_udp_bucket = inet_udp_port_hash[h]
        inet_udp_port_hash[h] = idx

Remove socket `idx` from its bucket. Walks the chain to find the
predecessor, splices the link past `idx`, and clears `next`. No-op
if `idx` isn't actually in the bucket — happens when `pid_exit`
sweeps a socket that close() already cleaned up.

    inet_udp_hash_remove(idx: int)
        val h = inet_udp_bucket(inet_sockets[idx].bound_port)
        var cur = inet_udp_port_hash[h]
        if cur == idx
            inet_udp_port_hash[h] = inet_sockets[idx].next_in_udp_bucket
            inet_sockets[idx].next_in_udp_bucket = -1
            return
        while cur >= 0
            val nx = inet_sockets[cur].next_in_udp_bucket
            if nx == idx
                inet_sockets[cur].next_in_udp_bucket = inet_sockets[idx].next_in_udp_bucket
                inet_sockets[idx].next_in_udp_bucket = -1
                return
            cur = nx


Phase 2 of the net-stack quality plan: count UDP slots whose
`owner_tid` matches `tid`.  Companion to `inet_count_tcp_owner`
in inet_proto.lsysl; callers add the two for the full per-tid
usage.  Lives in this file because cross-file VAR reads break
the analyzer (see feedback_sysl_syntax_gotchas #19); cross-file
FN calls are fine, hence the split.

    inet_count_udp_owner(tid: int) -> int
        var n = 0
        var i = 0
        while i < INET_MAX_SOCKETS
            if inet_sockets[i].in_use != 0
                if inet_sockets[i].owner_tid == tid
                    n += 1
            i += 1
        n


RFC 5227 ARP probe state. `arp_probe_target` is the IPv4
address currently being probed (0 = no probe active).
`arp_probe_seen` is set by the ARP RX path when any ARP
request OR reply arrives whose sender IP matches
`arp_probe_target` and whose sender MAC is not our own (NIC
loopback guard per §2.1.1). The dhclient-side probe loop
clears both fields via the CHECK op when the probe window
ends.

    var arp_probe_target: u32 = u32(0)
    var arp_probe_seen:   int = 0


Returns 1 if `frame` is an ARP packet whose sender IP matches
the probe target AND whose sender MAC is not our own. Returns
0 otherwise (including when no probe is active, when the
target doesn't match, or when the frame came from our own MAC
via NIC loopback). Used by the ARP RX path to flag defenders.

    inet_arp_probe_matches(frame: *byte) -> int
        if arp_probe_target == u32(0)
            return 0
        val sender_ip = ipv4(
            int(frame[14 + 14]) & 0xff,
            int(frame[14 + 15]) & 0xff,
            int(frame[14 + 16]) & 0xff,
            int(frame[14 + 17]) & 0xff)
        if sender_ip != arp_probe_target
            return 0
        // Sender MAC lives at frame+14+8..13 (ARP SHA field).
        // Compare against our own MAC to filter out NIC loopback.
        var i = 0
        var same = 1
        while i < 6
            if frame[14 + 8 + i] != inet_our_mac[i]
                same = 0
            i += 1
        if same != 0
            return 0
        1


### Byte-buffer helpers

Read / write the big-endian u16s used in the wire protocol. No
alignment assumptions — pointers come from IPC buffers.

    inet_read_u16(p: *byte, off: int) -> int
        ((int(p[off]) & 0xff) << 8) | (int(p[off + 1]) & 0xff)

    inet_write_u16(p: *byte, off: int, v: int)
        p[off]     = byte((v >> 8) & 0xff)
        p[off + 1] = byte(v & 0xff)

    inet_read_ipv4(p: *byte, off: int) -> u32
        ipv4(int(p[off]), int(p[off + 1]), int(p[off + 2]), int(p[off + 3]))

    inet_write_ipv4(p: *byte, off: int, ip: u32)
        p[off]     = byte(ipv4_octet(ip, 0))
        p[off + 1] = byte(ipv4_octet(ip, 1))
        p[off + 2] = byte(ipv4_octet(ip, 2))
        p[off + 3] = byte(ipv4_octet(ip, 3))


Initialize the socket table once. Called from the server main
loop before `port_register` so sockets are consistent before any
client can reach us.

    inet_init_sockets()
        var i = 0
        while i < INET_MAX_SOCKETS
            inet_sockets[i].in_use = 0
            inet_sockets[i].pending_tid = -1
            inet_sockets[i].recv_deadline = 0
            inet_sockets[i].pending_error = 0
            inet_sockets[i].next_in_udp_bucket = -1
            i += 1
        inet_udp_hash_init()
        // Note: `inet_udp_fire_seq` (in inet_proto.lsysl) is left at
        // its default-zeroed state. The shim re-syncs `last_seen_seq`
        // on every EPOLL_CTL_ADD anyway, so a recycled slot's stale
        // counter is harmless — first scan treats it as the live
        // baseline.


### Locate a bound socket

Hash-bucket walk on `port`. Phase 3 chunk 2 — replaces the prior
linear scan over all 32 slots. Average bucket length is ~2 at
INET_MAX_SOCKETS=32 / INET_UDP_HASH_N=16; worst case (all 32 sockets
collide to one bucket) is still O(N) but only ever realized by
bind-by-port-mod-16 patterns the tests don't exercise.

    inet_find_bound(ip: u32, port: int) -> int
        var i = inet_udp_port_hash[inet_udp_bucket(port)]
        while i >= 0
            if inet_sockets[i].in_use != 0
                if inet_sockets[i].bound != 0
                    if inet_sockets[i].bound_port == port
                        val bi = inet_sockets[i].bound_ip
                        if bi == INADDR_ANY
                            return i
                        if bi == ip
                            return i
            i = inet_sockets[i].next_in_udp_bucket
        -1


### Allocate an ephemeral source port

Linear scan from 49152 upward (RFC 6335 suggested range), returning
the first port no existing socket is bound to. Returns -1 if the
entire range is occupied — won't happen in practice with 32 sockets
but the guard keeps the loop bounded.

    inet_alloc_ephemeral_port() -> int
        var port = 49152
        while port < 65536
            if inet_find_bound_port(port) < 0
                return port
            port += 1
        -1


### Locate any socket bound to `port`

Port-only variant for UDP RX dispatch. The guest has a single unicast
IPv4 address plus `INADDR_ANY`, so IP matching on inbound packets
would just restate what's already true (the nic delivered the frame
to our MAC). Returning the first port match keeps the RX path simple
and matches how typical single-homed stacks behave.

    inet_find_bound_port(port: int) -> int
        var i = inet_udp_port_hash[inet_udp_bucket(port)]
        while i >= 0
            if inet_sockets[i].in_use != 0
                if inet_sockets[i].bound != 0
                    if inet_sockets[i].bound_port == port
                        return i
            i = inet_sockets[i].next_in_udp_bucket
        -1


### Enqueue an incoming datagram

Returns 1 on success, 0 if the queue is full (caller drops the
packet per UDP semantics).

    inet_enqueue(fd: int, src_ip: u32, src_port: int, data: *byte, dlen: int) -> int
        if inet_sockets[fd].q_count >= INET_MAX_QUEUE
            return 0
        val slot = inet_sockets[fd].q_tail
        inet_sockets[fd].queue[slot].src_ip = src_ip
        inet_sockets[fd].queue[slot].src_port = src_port
        var copy_len = dlen
        if copy_len > INET_MAX_DATA
            copy_len = INET_MAX_DATA
        var i = 0
        while i < copy_len
            inet_sockets[fd].queue[slot].data[i] = data[i]
            i += 1
        inet_sockets[fd].queue[slot].data_len = copy_len
        inet_sockets[fd].q_tail = (slot + 1) % INET_MAX_QUEUE
        inet_sockets[fd].q_count += 1
        inet_epoll_fire(1, fd)
        1


### Deliver a datagram directly to a socket's pending recvfrom

Used by both the loopback sendto path and the UDP RX path. Assumes
`inet_sockets[fd].pending_tid >= 0`. Clears the waiter slot, builds
the recvfrom reply (same shape as `inet_deliver_head` produces), and
replies via IPC.

    inet_deliver_to_waiter(fd: int, src_ip: u32, src_port: int, data: *byte, dlen: int)
        val waiter = inet_sockets[fd].pending_tid
        val mlen = inet_sockets[fd].pending_maxlen
        inet_sockets[fd].pending_tid = -1
        inet_sockets[fd].recv_deadline = 0
        val widx = inet_frame_alloc()
        val wr = inet_frame_buf(widx)
        var deliver_len = dlen
        if deliver_len > mlen
            deliver_len = mlen
        wr[0] = 0
        wr[1] = byte(AF_INET & 0xff)
        inet_write_u16(wr, 2, src_port)
        inet_write_ipv4(wr, 4, src_ip)
        inet_write_u16(wr, 8, deliver_len)
        var i = 0
        while i < deliver_len
            wr[10 + i] = data[i]
            i += 1
        ipc_reply(waiter, wr, 10 + deliver_len)
        inet_frame_release(widx)


### Deliver one queued datagram to a pending recvfrom

Writes the recvfrom reply into `reply` and returns its length.
Only called when we know there's both a queued dgram and a
waiter. `maxlen` caps how many payload bytes we hand back.

    inet_deliver_head(fd: int, maxlen: int, reply: *byte) -> int
        val slot = inet_sockets[fd].q_head
        val src_ip = inet_sockets[fd].queue[slot].src_ip
        val src_port = inet_sockets[fd].queue[slot].src_port
        var dlen = inet_sockets[fd].queue[slot].data_len
        if dlen > maxlen
            dlen = maxlen
        reply[0] = 0
        reply[1] = byte(AF_INET & 0xff)
        inet_write_u16(reply, 2, src_port)
        inet_write_ipv4(reply, 4, src_ip)
        inet_write_u16(reply, 8, dlen)
        var i = 0
        while i < dlen
            reply[10 + i] = inet_sockets[fd].queue[slot].data[i]
            i += 1
        inet_sockets[fd].q_head = (slot + 1) % INET_MAX_QUEUE
        inet_sockets[fd].q_count -= 1
        10 + dlen


### SOCKET handler

    inet_handle_socket(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val family = int(buf[1]) & 0xff
        val stype  = int(buf[2]) & 0xff
        if family != AF_INET
            var err: [2]byte
            err[0] = byte(-1)
            err[1] = 0
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 2)
            return
        if stype != SOCK_DGRAM
            var err: [2]byte
            err[0] = byte(-1)
            err[1] = 0
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 2)
            return
        // Phase 2 net-stack quality plan: per-tid cap on UDP
        // slots.  Literal 8 — duplicated in `begin_connect` /
        // `begin_listen` (inet_proto.lsysl) for the TCP pool.
        // Sysl analyzer bug #19 (cross-file val + cross-file fn
        // refs both break analysis) is what forces the per-pool
        // split; a true combined cap would need a fix to the
        // analyzer.
        if inet_count_udp_owner(sender) >= 8
            var err: [2]byte
            err[0] = byte(-1)
            err[1] = 0
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 2)
            return
        var idx = -1
        var i = 0
        while i < INET_MAX_SOCKETS
            if inet_sockets[i].in_use == 0
                idx = i
                i = INET_MAX_SOCKETS
            else
                i += 1
        if idx < 0
            var err: [2]byte
            err[0] = byte(-1)
            err[1] = 0
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 2)
            return
        inet_sockets[idx].in_use = 1
        inet_sockets[idx].owner_tid = sender
        inet_sockets[idx].bound = 0
        inet_sockets[idx].bound_ip = INADDR_ANY
        inet_sockets[idx].bound_port = 0
        inet_sockets[idx].q_head = 0
        inet_sockets[idx].q_tail = 0
        inet_sockets[idx].q_count = 0
        inet_sockets[idx].pending_tid = -1
        inet_sockets[idx].recv_deadline = 0
        inet_sockets[idx].pending_error = 0
        inet_sockets[idx].next_in_udp_bucket = -1
        // No need to reset inet_udp_fire_seq[idx] — see
        // `inet_init_sockets` for why a stale counter is harmless.
        var reply: [2]byte
        reply[0] = 0
        reply[1] = byte(idx & 0xff)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 2)


Validate an fd belongs to `sender`. Rejects out-of-range fds,
free slots, and slots whose `owner_tid` points at a different
thread — the latter is the ownership check that prevents process
A from issuing IPC with process B's inet fd (A would need to
guess the fd number today, but the guard makes the surface
explicit regardless). Returns 1 if OK, 0 otherwise. Call sites
pass the IPC `sender` argument.

    inet_fd_valid(fd: int, sender: int) -> int
        if fd < 0
            return 0
        if fd >= INET_MAX_SOCKETS
            return 0
        if inet_sockets[fd].in_use == 0
            return 0
        if inet_sockets[fd].owner_tid != sender
            return 0
        1


### BIND handler

    inet_handle_bind(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val fd = int(buf[1]) & 0xff
        if inet_fd_valid(fd, sender) == 0
            var err: [1]byte
            err[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 1)
            return
        val family = int(buf[2]) & 0xff
        if family != AF_INET
            var err: [1]byte
            err[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 1)
            return
        val port = inet_read_u16(buf, 3)
        val ip = inet_read_ipv4(buf, 5)
        if port <= 0
            var err: [1]byte
            err[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 1)
            return
        if ip != INADDR_ANY
            if ip != INADDR_LOOPBACK
                var err: [1]byte
                err[0] = byte(-1)
                ipc_reply(sender, err, 1)
                return
        if inet_find_bound(ip, port) >= 0
            var err: [1]byte
            err[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 1)
            return
        inet_sockets[fd].bound = 1
        inet_sockets[fd].bound_ip = ip
        inet_sockets[fd].bound_port = port
        inet_udp_hash_insert(fd)
        var reply: [1]byte
        reply[0] = 0
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)


### SENDTO handler

Loopback (`INADDR_LOOPBACK`) is delivered in-process against the
socket table. Non-loopback destinations go out the wire via
`inet_send_udp` — requires the socket to be bound so we have a valid
source port, and requires the gateway MAC to have been learned via
boot ARP. Either failure reports `status=-1, nsent=0`; UDP semantics
let us drop unmatched loopback targets silently with
`status=0, nsent=dlen`.

    inet_handle_sendto(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val fd = int(buf[1]) & 0xff
        if inet_fd_valid(fd, sender) == 0
            var err: [3]byte
            err[0] = byte(-1)
            err[1] = 0
            err[2] = 0
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 3)
            return
        val family = int(buf[2]) & 0xff
        if family != AF_INET
            var err: [3]byte
            err[0] = byte(-1)
            err[1] = 0
            err[2] = 0
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 3)
            return
        val dst_port = inet_read_u16(buf, 3)
        val dst_ip = inet_read_ipv4(buf, 5)
        val dlen = inet_read_u16(buf, 9)
        val data: *byte = buf + 11

Auto-bind unbound sockets to an ephemeral src port (matches POSIX
`sendto(2)` semantics). Covers both loopback and wire paths so
receivers always see a non-zero `src_port` in `recvfrom` replies.
Fails sendto only if the ephemeral range is exhausted — 32 sockets
vs. 16K ports, so not in practice.

        if inet_sockets[fd].bound == 0
            val eph = inet_alloc_ephemeral_port()
            if eph < 0
                var err: [3]byte
                err[0] = byte(-1)
                err[1] = 0
                err[2] = 0
                ipc_reply(sender, err, 3)
                return
            inet_sockets[fd].bound = 1
            inet_sockets[fd].bound_ip = INADDR_ANY
            inet_sockets[fd].bound_port = eph
            inet_udp_hash_insert(fd)
        val src_port = inet_sockets[fd].bound_port
        // Loopback fastpath: any address in 127/8 or our own external
        // IP routes to a local socket via the in-memory queue, never
        // touching the NIC. The receiver always sees src_ip = 127.0.0.1
        // so a connect()ed local UDP fd can match its peer regardless
        // of which loopback address the sender used.
        if inet_is_loopback_ip(dst_ip) != 0
            val target = inet_find_bound(dst_ip, dst_port)
            if target >= 0
                if inet_sockets[target].pending_tid >= 0
                    inet_deliver_to_waiter(target, INADDR_LOOPBACK, src_port, data, dlen)
                else
                    inet_enqueue(target, INADDR_LOOPBACK, src_port, data, dlen)
            var reply: [3]byte
            reply[0] = 0
            inet_write_u16(reply, 1, dlen)
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 3)
            return
        val r = inet_send_udp(sender, dst_ip, dst_port, src_port, data, dlen)
        if r < 0
            var err: [3]byte
            err[0] = byte(-1)
            err[1] = 0
            err[2] = 0
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 3)
            return
        var reply: [3]byte
        reply[0] = 0
        inet_write_u16(reply, 1, dlen)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 3)


### RECVFROM handler

If the socket already has queued datagrams, deliver the head
immediately. Otherwise park the sender's thread id in
`pending_tid` and return without replying — the reply is
produced later when a matching sendto arrives.

Only one pending recv per socket. A second recv while one is
already pending is rejected. Good enough for single-threaded
programs; multi-waiter support is on the list.

    inet_handle_recvfrom(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val fd = int(buf[1]) & 0xff
        if inet_fd_valid(fd, sender) == 0
            var err: [10]byte
            err[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 10)
            return
        val maxlen_raw = inet_read_u16(buf, 2)
        var maxlen = maxlen_raw
        if maxlen > INET_MAX_DATA
            maxlen = INET_MAX_DATA
        // Pending ICMP error wins over queued data — Linux returns
        // -ECONNREFUSED on the next recv() and only then resumes
        // delivering normal datagrams. We mirror that.
        if inet_sockets[fd].pending_error != 0
            inet_sockets[fd].pending_error = 0
            var er: [10]byte
            er[0] = byte(2)
            ipc_reply(sender, er, 10)
            return
        if inet_sockets[fd].q_count > 0
            val ridx = inet_frame_alloc()
            val reply = inet_frame_buf(ridx)
            val n = inet_deliver_head(fd, maxlen, reply)
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, n)
            inet_frame_release(ridx)
            return
        if inet_sockets[fd].pending_tid >= 0
            var err: [10]byte
            err[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 10)
            return
        inet_sockets[fd].pending_tid = sender
        inet_sockets[fd].pending_maxlen = maxlen
        // No reply — deferred until a dgram arrives or close.


### RECVFROM_TIMEOUT handler

Same as RECVFROM but with a bounded wait. Request layout:
`cmd(1) + fd(1) + maxlen(2) + timeout_ms(4)` = 8 bytes. The
reply shape matches RECVFROM so callers can use the same parser.

Status byte in the reply distinguishes outcomes:
- `0` — data delivered
- `1` — timed out (no datagram within `timeout_ms`)
- `-1` — error

`inet_udp_scan_timeouts()` in the server main loop replies with
status=1 when `now >= recv_deadline`. The deadline is expressed
in scheduler ticks (100 Hz → 10 ms each). We round timeout up so
a `1 ms` request still fires on the next tick rather than
possibly firing immediately.

    inet_handle_recvfrom_timeout(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val fd = int(buf[1]) & 0xff
        if inet_fd_valid(fd, sender) == 0
            var err: [10]byte
            err[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 10)
            return
        val maxlen_raw = inet_read_u16(buf, 2)
        var maxlen = maxlen_raw
        if maxlen > INET_MAX_DATA
            maxlen = INET_MAX_DATA
        val tms_hi = inet_read_u16(buf, 4)
        val tms_lo = inet_read_u16(buf, 6)
        val timeout_ms = (tms_hi << 16) | tms_lo
        if inet_sockets[fd].pending_error != 0
            inet_sockets[fd].pending_error = 0
            var er: [10]byte
            er[0] = byte(2)
            ipc_reply(sender, er, 10)
            return
        if inet_sockets[fd].q_count > 0
            val ridx = inet_frame_alloc()
            val reply = inet_frame_buf(ridx)
            val n = inet_deliver_head(fd, maxlen, reply)
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, n)
            inet_frame_release(ridx)
            return
        if inet_sockets[fd].pending_tid >= 0
            var err: [10]byte
            err[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 10)
            return
        if timeout_ms <= 0
            var tr: [10]byte
            tr[0] = byte(1)
            ipc_reply(sender, tr, 10)
            return
        val ticks = (timeout_ms + 9) / 10
        inet_sockets[fd].pending_tid = sender
        inet_sockets[fd].pending_maxlen = maxlen
        inet_sockets[fd].recv_deadline = int(uptime()) + ticks
        // No reply — deferred until a dgram arrives, timeout
        // expires, or close.


### TCP_DEBUG handler

Expose internal congestion-control state for a TCP socket so
the test harness can assert cwnd / ssthresh / recovery-state
behaviour without having to reverse-engineer it from timing.
Read-only — no state is mutated.

```
request: cmd(1) + fd(2)                 = 3 bytes
reply:   status(1) + state(1)
       + cwnd(4)          + ssthresh(4)
       + snd_una(4)       + snd_nxt(4)
       + snd_wnd(4)       + send_len(4)
       + fast_recovery(4) + dup_ack_count(4)
       + sack_ok(4)       + rexmit_count(4)     = 42 bytes
```

    inet_handle_tcp_debug(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val fd = inet_read_u16(buf, 1) & 0xffff
        var reply: [42]byte
        if inet_tcp_slot_owned(fd, sender) == 0
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 42)
            return
        reply[0] = 0
        reply[1] = byte(tcp_sockets[fd].state & 0xff)
        inet_dbg_write_i32(reply, 2,  tcp_sockets[fd].snd_cwnd)
        inet_dbg_write_i32(reply, 6,  tcp_sockets[fd].snd_ssthresh)
        inet_dbg_write_i32(reply, 10, int(tcp_sockets[fd].snd_una))
        inet_dbg_write_i32(reply, 14, int(tcp_sockets[fd].snd_nxt))
        inet_dbg_write_i32(reply, 18, tcp_sockets[fd].snd_wnd)
        inet_dbg_write_i32(reply, 22, tcp_sockets[fd].send_len)
        inet_dbg_write_i32(reply, 26, tcp_sockets[fd].fast_recovery)
        inet_dbg_write_i32(reply, 30, tcp_sockets[fd].dup_ack_count)
        inet_dbg_write_i32(reply, 34, tcp_sockets[fd].sack_ok)
        inet_dbg_write_i32(reply, 38, tcp_sockets[fd].rexmit_count)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 42)

    inet_dbg_write_i32(p: *byte, off: int, v: int)
        p[off]     = byte((v >> 24) & 0xff)
        p[off + 1] = byte((v >> 16) & 0xff)
        p[off + 2] = byte((v >>  8) & 0xff)
        p[off + 3] = byte( v        & 0xff)


### ARP probe handler

Four-operation command driven by dhclient for RFC 5227
conflict detection.

```
request: cmd(1) + op(1) + target_ip(4)   = 6 bytes
reply:   status(1) + seen(1)             = 2 bytes
```

`seen` is meaningful only for `CHECK` and is 1 if any
defender was observed since the preceding `START`.

    inet_handle_arp_probe(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val op = int(buf[1]) & 0xff
        val target_ip = ipv4(
            int(buf[2]) & 0xff,
            int(buf[3]) & 0xff,
            int(buf[4]) & 0xff,
            int(buf[5]) & 0xff)
        var reply: [2]byte
        reply[0] = 0
        reply[1] = 0
        if op == ARP_PROBE_OP_START
            arp_probe_target = target_ip
            arp_probe_seen = 0
        else if op == ARP_PROBE_OP_SEND
            val rc = inet_send_arp_probe(
                ipv4_octet(target_ip, 0),
                ipv4_octet(target_ip, 1),
                ipv4_octet(target_ip, 2),
                ipv4_octet(target_ip, 3))
            if rc < 0
                reply[0] = byte(-1)
        else if op == ARP_PROBE_OP_CHECK
            reply[1] = byte(arp_probe_seen & 0xff)
            arp_probe_target = u32(0)
            arp_probe_seen = 0
        else if op == ARP_PROBE_OP_ANNOUNCE
            val arc = inet_send_arp_announce(
                ipv4_octet(target_ip, 0),
                ipv4_octet(target_ip, 1),
                ipv4_octet(target_ip, 2),
                ipv4_octet(target_ip, 3))
            if arc < 0
                reply[0] = byte(-1)
        else
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 2)


### ICMP echo send / check

`ping`-style: the client registers a ping (id, seq) and asks us
to emit an ECHO_REQUEST to `dst_ip`; later it polls for a
matching ECHO_REPLY. Both operations are non-blocking: SEND
returns immediately after transmit, CHECK returns "still
waiting" if no reply has arrived yet. ARP misses are surfaced
to the caller so it can retry.

```
SEND request:  cmd(1) + dst_ip(4) + id(2) + seq(2)   = 9 bytes
SEND reply:    status(1)                             = 1 byte
CHECK request: cmd(1) + id(2) + seq(2)               = 5 bytes
CHECK reply:   status(1) + src_ip(4) + rtt(4)        = 9 bytes
    status: 0 = reply arrived, 1 = still waiting, -1 = no slot
```

    inet_handle_icmp_send(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val dst_ip = ipv4(
            int(buf[1]) & 0xff,
            int(buf[2]) & 0xff,
            int(buf[3]) & 0xff,
            int(buf[4]) & 0xff)
        val id  = (int(buf[5]) & 0xff) << 8 | (int(buf[6]) & 0xff)
        val seq = (int(buf[7]) & 0xff) << 8 | (int(buf[8]) & 0xff)
        var reply: [1]byte
        reply[0] = 0
        if inet_ping_register(sender, id, seq, int(uptime())) < 0
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)
            return
        if inet_send_icmp_echo_to(dst_ip, id, seq) < 0
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)


    inet_handle_icmp_check(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val id  = (int(buf[1]) & 0xff) << 8 | (int(buf[2]) & 0xff)
        val seq = (int(buf[3]) & 0xff) << 8 | (int(buf[4]) & 0xff)
        var reply: [9]byte
        var k = 0
        while k < 9
            reply[k] = 0
            k += 1
        var src_ip: u32 = u32(0)
        var rtt: int = 0
        val rc = inet_ping_check(id, seq, &src_ip, &rtt)
        if rc < 0
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
        else if rc == 0
            reply[0] = 1
        else
            reply[0] = 0
            reply[1] = byte(ipv4_octet(src_ip, 0))
            reply[2] = byte(ipv4_octet(src_ip, 1))
            reply[3] = byte(ipv4_octet(src_ip, 2))
            reply[4] = byte(ipv4_octet(src_ip, 3))
            reply[5] = byte((rtt >> 24) & 0xff)
            reply[6] = byte((rtt >> 16) & 0xff)
            reply[7] = byte((rtt >> 8) & 0xff)
            reply[8] = byte(rtt & 0xff)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 9)


### UDP recv-timeout scanner

Called from the inet main loop on every wake. Scans every
socket with a pending recvfrom that has a non-zero deadline, and
when the deadline has passed replies with status=1 (timeout). We
clear the waiter before replying so a datagram arriving on the
same tick doesn't wake the thread twice.

Timer wakes happen every ~50 ms (`svc_timer_subscribe(5)` in
inet_server), so worst-case overshoot on an idle port is one
tick period. Good enough for DHCP-scale retransmits.

    inet_udp_scan_timeouts()
        val now = int(uptime())
        var i = 0
        while i < INET_MAX_SOCKETS
            if inet_sockets[i].in_use != 0
                if inet_sockets[i].pending_tid >= 0
                    val dl = inet_sockets[i].recv_deadline
                    if dl != 0
                        if now >= dl
                            val waiter = inet_sockets[i].pending_tid
                            inet_sockets[i].pending_tid = -1
                            inet_sockets[i].recv_deadline = 0
                            var tr: [10]byte
                            tr[0] = byte(1)
                            ipc_reply(waiter, tr, 10)
            i += 1


### CLOSE handler

Closes the slot. If a recv was pending, reply with an error so
the caller doesn't block forever.

    inet_handle_close(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val fd = int(buf[1]) & 0xff
        if inet_fd_valid(fd, sender) == 0
            var err: [1]byte
            err[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 1)
            return
        if inet_sockets[fd].pending_tid >= 0
            val waiter = inet_sockets[fd].pending_tid
            inet_sockets[fd].pending_tid = -1
            inet_sockets[fd].recv_deadline = 0
            var wr: [10]byte
            wr[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(waiter, wr, 10)
        if inet_sockets[fd].bound != 0
            inet_udp_hash_remove(fd)
        inet_sockets[fd].in_use = 0
        inet_sockets[fd].bound = 0
        inet_sockets[fd].q_count = 0
        inet_sockets[fd].q_head = 0
        inet_sockets[fd].q_tail = 0
        inet_sockets[fd].pending_error = 0
        var reply: [1]byte
        reply[0] = 0
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)


### TCP command handlers

All four commands are blocking: they do NOT reply before the
wakeup condition is met. Handlers stash the caller tid in the
per-socket waiter slot (connect_waiter / recv_waiter / close_waiter)
and the state machine / timer scan delivers the reply later.
SEND is the one exception — POSIX `write()` semantics mean it
replies as soon as bytes land in the send buffer.

Wire layout (all big-endian u16):
- TCP_CONNECT: cmd(1), ip(4), port(2) -> status(1)
- TCP_SEND: cmd(1), fd(2), len(2), data(len) -> status(1), written(2)
- TCP_RECV: cmd(1), fd(2), maxlen(2) -> status(1), len(2), data(len); len=0 means EOF
- TCP_CLOSE: cmd(1), fd(2) -> status(1)

`fd` is a 16-bit raw socket index. First byte at offset 1 is the
high byte (0), second (offset 2) is the low byte / index.

    inet_read_u16_at(buf: *byte, off: int) -> int
        inet_read_u16(buf, off)

    inet_handle_tcp_connect(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val ip = inet_read_ipv4(buf, 1)
        val port = inet_read_u16(buf, 5)
        val sndbuf = inet_read_u16(buf, 7)
        val rcvbuf = inet_read_u16(buf, 9)
        val s = inet_tcp_begin_connect(sender, sender, ip, port, sndbuf, rcvbuf)
        if s < 0
            var err: [2]byte
            err[0] = byte(-1)
            err[1] = 0
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 2)
            return
        // Deferred reply — inet_tcp_state_step replies when ESTABLISHED.

Non-blocking connect. Allocate slot + emit SYN like the
blocking variant, but pass `-1` for `caller_tid` so the slot's
connect_waiter is the unset sentinel and the SYN-ACK path
won't try to deliver an IPC reply. Reply immediately with the
slot fd; the shim translates that to `-EINPROGRESS` for the
user. EPOLLOUT delivery once ESTABLISHED comes through the
existing `inet_epoll_fire(2, s)` at SYN_SENT → ESTABLISHED.

    inet_handle_tcp_connect_nb(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val ip = inet_read_ipv4(buf, 1)
        val port = inet_read_u16(buf, 5)
        val sndbuf = inet_read_u16(buf, 7)
        val rcvbuf = inet_read_u16(buf, 9)
        val s = inet_tcp_begin_connect(sender, -1, ip, port, sndbuf, rcvbuf)
        var reply: [2]byte
        if s < 0
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
            reply[1] = 0
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 2)
            return
        reply[0] = byte(0)
        reply[1] = byte(s & 0xff)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 2)

    inet_handle_tcp_send(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val fd = inet_read_u16(buf, 1) & 0xffff
        val dlen = inet_read_u16(buf, 3)
        val data: *byte = buf + 5
        if inet_tcp_slot_owned(fd, sender) == 0
            var err: [3]byte
            err[0] = byte(-1)
            inet_write_u16(err, 1, 0)
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 3)
            return
        val n = inet_tcp_do_send(fd, data, dlen)
        if n < 0
            var err: [3]byte
            err[0] = byte(-1)
            inet_write_u16(err, 1, 0)
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 3)
            return
        // Park caller when send_buf is full and they wanted bytes.
        // Recv loop stays unblocked; ACK drain wakes via
        // inet_tcp_drain_send_waiter with reply (status=0,
        // written=0).  Shim retries the same chunk against the
        // freshly opened window.  Single-waiter for now —
        // concurrent senders on the same fd reply 0 immediately
        // and the shim retries (busy-loop, but only when two
        // threads share an fd, which POSIX rarely promises).
        if n == 0 && dlen > 0 && tcp_sockets[fd].send_waiter < 0
            tcp_sockets[fd].send_waiter = sender
            tcp_sockets[fd].send_waiter_len = dlen
            return
        var reply: [3]byte
        reply[0] = 0
        inet_write_u16(reply, 1, n)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 3)

    inet_handle_tcp_recv(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val fd = inet_read_u16(buf, 1) & 0xffff
        val maxlen_raw = inet_read_u16(buf, 3)
        var maxlen = maxlen_raw
        if maxlen > 500
            maxlen = 500
        if inet_tcp_slot_owned(fd, sender) == 0
            var err: [3]byte
            err[0] = byte(-1)
            inet_write_u16(err, 1, 0)
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 3)
            return
        tcp_sockets[fd].recv_waiter = sender
        tcp_sockets[fd].recv_maxlen = maxlen
        if tcp_sockets[fd].recv_len > 0
            var give = tcp_sockets[fd].recv_len
            if give > maxlen
                give = maxlen
            inet_tcp_reply_recv(fd, give)
            return
        if tcp_sockets[fd].peer_fin != 0
            inet_tcp_reply_recv(fd, 0)
            return
        // Deferred reply — state step delivers when data arrives.

Non-blocking TCP recv. Same wire format as TCP_RECV (cmd + fd +
maxlen). Three reply shapes:
  - data ready → status=0 + len + data (same as TCP_RECV).
  - peer FIN  → status=0 + len=0 (EOF, same as TCP_RECV).
  - no data   → status=-11 (-EAGAIN, 3 bytes).
Invalid fd / closed socket still produces the -1 error reply.
Unlike TCP_RECV this never parks the caller; the shim picks
the NB variant when `O_NONBLOCK` is set on the fd.

    inet_handle_tcp_recv_nb(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val fd = inet_read_u16(buf, 1) & 0xffff
        val maxlen_raw = inet_read_u16(buf, 3)
        var maxlen = maxlen_raw
        if maxlen > 500
            maxlen = 500
        if inet_tcp_slot_owned(fd, sender) == 0
            var err: [3]byte
            err[0] = byte(-1)
            inet_write_u16(err, 1, 0)
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 3)
            return
        if tcp_sockets[fd].recv_len > 0
            tcp_sockets[fd].recv_waiter = sender
            tcp_sockets[fd].recv_maxlen = maxlen
            var give = tcp_sockets[fd].recv_len
            if give > maxlen
                give = maxlen
            inet_tcp_reply_recv(fd, give)
            return
        if tcp_sockets[fd].peer_fin != 0
            tcp_sockets[fd].recv_waiter = sender
            tcp_sockets[fd].recv_maxlen = maxlen
            inet_tcp_reply_recv(fd, 0)
            return
        var eagain: [3]byte
        eagain[0] = byte(-11)
        inet_write_u16(eagain, 1, 0)
        ipc_reply(sender, eagain, 3)

Non-consuming readiness query for the shim's epoll
implementation. Caller passes (kind, fd) and gets back a
`ready_mask` packed into the reply: bit 0 = readable, bit 2
= writable, bit 3 = error, bit 4 = hangup. Same bit positions
as Linux's `EPOLLIN` / `EPOLLOUT` / `EPOLLERR` / `EPOLLHUP`.

Wire: [cmd(1), kind(1), fd(u16)] = 4 bytes.
Reply: [status(1), mask(u16), fire_seq(u32 le)] = 7 bytes.

`fire_seq` is the per-fd monotonic counter that
`inet_epoll_fire` bumps on every readiness edge — the shim
caches it per `epoll_table` entry and clears that entry's
`last_reported` only when the seq advances, so EPOLLET
subscribers don't over-fire on unrelated fds in the same
instance.

kind: 1 = UDP, 2 = TCP (listen sockets are detected by state).

For UDP, readable iff there's a queued datagram; writable
always. For TCP regular sockets, readable iff recv_len > 0 or
peer_fin (so an EOF read won't block); writable iff there is
send-buffer space and we're connected; hangup iff peer_fin.
For TCP listen sockets, readable iff the accept queue has a
ready child.

    inet_handle_poll(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val kind = int(buf[1]) & 0xff
        val fd = inet_read_u16(buf, 2) & 0xffff
        var mask = 0
        var seq = 0
        if kind == 1
            // UDP — owner check via inet_fd_valid which scopes
            // by sender PTBR/tid.
            if inet_fd_valid(fd, sender) != 0
                if inet_sockets[fd].q_count > 0
                    mask = mask | 0x1
                mask = mask | 0x4
                seq = inet_udp_fire_seq[fd]
        else if kind == 2
            if inet_tcp_slot_owned(fd, sender) != 0
                val st = tcp_sockets[fd].state
                if st == TCP_STATE_LISTEN
                    if tcp_sockets[fd].accept_head != tcp_sockets[fd].accept_tail
                        mask = mask | 0x1
                else
                    if tcp_sockets[fd].recv_len > 0
                        mask = mask | 0x1
                    if tcp_sockets[fd].peer_fin != 0
                        mask = mask | 0x1
                        mask = mask | 0x10
                    if st == TCP_STATE_ESTABLISHED || st == TCP_STATE_CLOSE_WAIT
                        if tcp_sockets[fd].send_len < tcp_sockets[fd].send_buf_size
                            mask = mask | 0x4
                    if st == TCP_STATE_CLOSED
                        mask = mask | 0x8
                        mask = mask | 0x10
                seq = tcp_sockets[fd].fire_seq
        var reply: [7]byte
        reply[0] = byte(0)
        inet_write_u16(reply, 1, mask)
        // fire_seq little-endian (matches the shim's epoll-event
        // serialization throughout `oskit.posix.shim`).
        reply[3] = byte(seq & 0xff)
        reply[4] = byte((seq >> 8) & 0xff)
        reply[5] = byte((seq >> 16) & 0xff)
        reply[6] = byte((seq >> 24) & 0xff)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 7)

shutdown(fd, how). `how` values: 0 = SHUT_RD, 1 = SHUT_WR,
2 = SHUT_RDWR. SHUT_RD is a no-op today — we don't track a
separate "read shut down" state, so the peer sees no effect and
subsequent recvs still deliver data. SHUT_WR emits our FIN via
`inet_tcp_do_shutdown_wr` without parking the caller. SHUT_RDWR
is treated as SHUT_WR (a proper SHUT_RDWR would also drop
buffered recv; deferred). Wire: cmd(1) + fd(u16) + how(u8) = 4.
Reply: status(1).

    inet_handle_tcp_shutdown(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val fd = inet_read_u16(buf, 1) & 0xffff
        val how = int(buf[3]) & 0xff
        var reply: [1]byte
        if inet_tcp_slot_owned(fd, sender) == 0
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)
            return
        if how != 0
            val _ = inet_tcp_do_shutdown_wr(fd)
        reply[0] = byte(0)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)

    inet_handle_tcp_close(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val fd = inet_read_u16(buf, 1) & 0xffff
        if inet_tcp_slot_owned(fd, sender) == 0
            var err: [1]byte
            err[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 1)
            return
        val r = inet_tcp_do_close(fd, sender)
        if r == 1
            var reply: [1]byte
            reply[0] = 0
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)
            return
        if r < 0
            var err: [1]byte
            err[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 1)
            return
        // Deferred reply — state step delivers when FIN is acked.

SO_KEEPALIVE on/off.  Wire: cmd(1) + fd(u16 BE) + on(1) = 4 bytes
in; reply: 1-byte status.  Inet enforces the probe/giveup cycle in
`inet_tcp_scan_timers` for sockets that are in ESTABLISHED or
CLOSE_WAIT with `keepalive_on != 0`.

    inet_handle_tcp_set_keepalive(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val fd = inet_read_u16(buf, 1) & 0xffff
        val on = int(buf[3]) & 0xff
        var reply: [1]byte
        if inet_tcp_slot_owned(fd, sender) == 0
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)
            return
        val rc = inet_tcp_set_keepalive(fd, on)
        reply[0] = byte(rc & 0xff)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)


setsockopt(IPPROTO_TCP, TCP_KEEPIDLE/INTVL/CNT) — set a per-socket
override.  Wire: cmd(1) + fd(u16 BE) + kind(1) + value(u32 BE)
= 8 bytes in; reply: 1-byte status.  `kind` = 0 (idle), 1 (intvl),
2 (cnt).  Idle/intvl arrive as ticks (shim has converted from
seconds at the boundary); cnt is a raw count.

    inet_handle_tcp_set_keepopt(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val fd = inet_read_u16(buf, 1) & 0xffff
        val kind = int(buf[3]) & 0xff
        val v_hi = inet_read_u16(buf, 4)
        val v_lo = inet_read_u16(buf, 6)
        val value = (v_hi << 16) | v_lo
        var reply: [1]byte
        if inet_tcp_slot_owned(fd, sender) == 0
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)
            return
        val rc = inet_tcp_set_keepopt(fd, kind, value)
        reply[0] = byte(rc & 0xff)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)


getsockopt(IPPROTO_TCP, TCP_KEEPIDLE/INTVL/CNT) — read the per-socket
override.  Wire: cmd(1) + fd(u16 BE) + kind(1) = 4 bytes in;
reply: status(1) + value(u32 BE) = 5 bytes.

    inet_handle_tcp_get_keepopt(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val fd = inet_read_u16(buf, 1) & 0xffff
        val kind = int(buf[3]) & 0xff
        var reply: [5]byte
        if inet_tcp_slot_owned(fd, sender) == 0
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 5)
            return
        val v = inet_tcp_get_keepopt(fd, kind)
        if v < 0
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 5)
            return
        reply[0] = 0
        reply[1] = byte((v >> 24) & 0xff)
        reply[2] = byte((v >> 16) & 0xff)
        reply[3] = byte((v >>  8) & 0xff)
        reply[4] = byte( v        & 0xff)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 5)


Read-and-clear the asynchronous-error channel for SO_ERROR.  Wire:
`cmd(1) + fd(u16) = 3` bytes.  Reply: `status(1) + errno(u8) = 2`
bytes — 0 errno means "no pending error".

This intentionally bypasses `inet_tcp_slot_owned`'s `in_use=0`
check: by the time the shim queries us, `close_slot` has almost
always already torn the slot down (that's the whole reason the
field exists).  We still gate on `owner_tid == sender` so a
different process can't read another's pending error in the
window before alloc reuses the slot — alloc resets the field, so
a same-pid reuse just sees 0, which is correct.

    inet_handle_tcp_get_err(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val fd = inet_read_u16(buf, 1) & 0xffff
        var reply: [2]byte
        if fd < 0 || fd >= TCP_MAX_SOCKETS
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
            reply[1] = byte(0)
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 2)
            return
        if tcp_sockets[fd].owner_tid != sender
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
            reply[1] = byte(0)
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 2)
            return
        val e = tcp_sockets[fd].pending_error
        tcp_sockets[fd].pending_error = 0
        reply[0] = byte(0)
        reply[1] = byte(e & 0xff)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 2)


Test-only: synthesize a SYN_SENT failure on whichever in_use slot
is currently waiting on `remote_port`.  Sets `pending_error = 111`
and runs `close_slot`, mirroring what an inbound RST in SYN_SENT
would do — without depending on slirp's RST timing.  Wire:
`cmd(1) + remote_port_be(u16) = 3` bytes.  Reply: 1-byte status
(0 = found-and-injected, -1 = no slot matched).

### inet_handle_tcp_get_info

Populates a Linux-compatible `struct tcp_info` (104 bytes) for
the slot. Field offsets and units (microseconds for RTT) match
what `getsockopt(IPPROTO_TCP, TCP_INFO)` returns on Linux so
libuv / Go-net-stack diagnostics that probe specific offsets
keep working. Fields we don't track (delivery-rate, fackets,
last_data_*, pmtu, rcv_rtt, etc.) stay zero.

State enum is mapped from our local `TCP_STATE_*` values
(0 = CLOSED, 1 = SYN_SENT, ...) to Linux's
(1 = ESTABLISHED, 2 = SYN_SENT, ...). The shim doesn't try to
interpret it — it's a number the user reads.

RTT/RTTvar conversion: our `srtt` / `rttvar` live in scheduler
ticks (10 ms). Linux reports tcpi_rtt in microseconds, so we
multiply by 10000.

Wire: cmd(1) + fd_be(2) = 3 bytes. Reply: status(1) + 104-byte
struct = 105 bytes. Status -1 if fd out of range or not owned.

    inet_handle_tcp_get_info(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val fd = inet_read_u16(buf, 1) & 0xffff
        var reply: [105]byte
        var z = 0
        while z < 105
            reply[z] = byte(0)
            z += 1
        if fd < 0 || fd >= TCP_MAX_SOCKETS
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 105)
            return
        if tcp_sockets[fd].owner_tid != sender
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 105)
            return
        if tcp_sockets[fd].in_use == 0
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 105)
            return
        // Map slix TCP_STATE_* → Linux's struct tcp_info state.
        var lstate = 0
        val s = tcp_sockets[fd].state
        if s == TCP_STATE_CLOSED
            lstate = 7
        else if s == TCP_STATE_SYN_SENT
            lstate = 2
        else if s == TCP_STATE_ESTABLISHED
            lstate = 1
        else if s == TCP_STATE_FIN_WAIT_1
            lstate = 4
        else if s == TCP_STATE_FIN_WAIT_2
            lstate = 5
        else if s == TCP_STATE_CLOSE_WAIT
            lstate = 8
        else if s == TCP_STATE_LAST_ACK
            lstate = 9
        else if s == TCP_STATE_TIME_WAIT
            lstate = 6
        else if s == TCP_STATE_LISTEN
            lstate = 10
        else if s == TCP_STATE_SYN_RCVD
            lstate = 3
        reply[0] = byte(0)                          // status
        // struct tcp_info begins at reply[1].  Offsets below are
        // relative to the struct base.
        reply[1 + 0] = byte(lstate & 0xff)          // tcpi_state @ 0
        reply[1 + 1] = byte(0)                      // tcpi_ca_state @ 1 (open)
        var rxmit = tcp_sockets[fd].retries
        if rxmit > 255
            rxmit = 255
        reply[1 + 2] = byte(rxmit & 0xff)           // tcpi_retransmits @ 2
        var probes = tcp_sockets[fd].keepalive_probes
        if probes > 255
            probes = 255
        reply[1 + 3] = byte(probes & 0xff)          // tcpi_probes @ 3
        reply[1 + 4] = byte(0)                      // tcpi_backoff @ 4
        // tcpi_options bitmap: bit 0 = TCPI_OPT_TIMESTAMPS,
        // bit 2 = TCPI_OPT_SACK, bit 3 = TCPI_OPT_WSCALE.
        var opts = 0
        if tcp_sockets[fd].ts_ok != 0
            opts = opts | 1
        if tcp_sockets[fd].sack_ok != 0
            opts = opts | 2
        if tcp_sockets[fd].snd_wscale != 0 || tcp_sockets[fd].rcv_wscale != 0
            opts = opts | 4
        reply[1 + 5] = byte(opts & 0xff)            // tcpi_options @ 5
        // Wscales packed: low 4 bits = snd, high 4 bits = rcv.
        val wscales = ((tcp_sockets[fd].snd_wscale & 0xf) | ((tcp_sockets[fd].rcv_wscale & 0xf) << 4))
        reply[1 + 6] = byte(wscales & 0xff)         // tcpi_*_wscale @ 6
        reply[1 + 7] = byte(0)                      // delivery_rate_app_limited @ 7
        // u32 fields. Inline little-endian writes — both arches
        // we target are LE and Linux's struct tcp_info is host-
        // endian, so this matches what real Linux apps expect.
        // tcpi_rto @ 8: our rto is in ticks; Linux uses
        // microseconds.  10ms/tick → 10000us/tick.
        val rto_us = tcp_sockets[fd].rto * 10000
        reply[1 +  8] = byte(rto_us & 0xff)
        reply[1 +  9] = byte((rto_us >> 8) & 0xff)
        reply[1 + 10] = byte((rto_us >> 16) & 0xff)
        reply[1 + 11] = byte((rto_us >> 24) & 0xff)
        // tcpi_ato @ 12 — delayed-ack timeout. We don't do
        // delayed ACKs, leave 0.
        // tcpi_snd_mss @ 16
        val smss = tcp_sockets[fd].mss
        reply[1 + 16] = byte(smss & 0xff)
        reply[1 + 17] = byte((smss >> 8) & 0xff)
        reply[1 + 18] = byte((smss >> 16) & 0xff)
        reply[1 + 19] = byte((smss >> 24) & 0xff)
        // tcpi_rcv_mss @ 20 — same.
        reply[1 + 20] = byte(smss & 0xff)
        reply[1 + 21] = byte((smss >> 8) & 0xff)
        reply[1 + 22] = byte((smss >> 16) & 0xff)
        reply[1 + 23] = byte((smss >> 24) & 0xff)
        // tcpi_unacked @ 24 — bytes in flight.
        val unacked = int(i32(tcp_sockets[fd].snd_nxt - tcp_sockets[fd].snd_una))
        reply[1 + 24] = byte(unacked & 0xff)
        reply[1 + 25] = byte((unacked >> 8) & 0xff)
        reply[1 + 26] = byte((unacked >> 16) & 0xff)
        reply[1 + 27] = byte((unacked >> 24) & 0xff)
        // tcpi_retrans @ 36 — retransmits in current burst.
        reply[1 + 36] = byte(tcp_sockets[fd].rexmit_count & 0xff)
        reply[1 + 37] = byte((tcp_sockets[fd].rexmit_count >> 8) & 0xff)
        reply[1 + 38] = byte((tcp_sockets[fd].rexmit_count >> 16) & 0xff)
        reply[1 + 39] = byte((tcp_sockets[fd].rexmit_count >> 24) & 0xff)
        // tcpi_rtt @ 68 in microseconds, tcpi_rttvar @ 72.
        val rtt_us = tcp_sockets[fd].srtt * 10000
        reply[1 + 68] = byte(rtt_us & 0xff)
        reply[1 + 69] = byte((rtt_us >> 8) & 0xff)
        reply[1 + 70] = byte((rtt_us >> 16) & 0xff)
        reply[1 + 71] = byte((rtt_us >> 24) & 0xff)
        val rttvar_us = tcp_sockets[fd].rttvar * 10000
        reply[1 + 72] = byte(rttvar_us & 0xff)
        reply[1 + 73] = byte((rttvar_us >> 8) & 0xff)
        reply[1 + 74] = byte((rttvar_us >> 16) & 0xff)
        reply[1 + 75] = byte((rttvar_us >> 24) & 0xff)
        // tcpi_snd_ssthresh @ 76, tcpi_snd_cwnd @ 80.
        reply[1 + 76] = byte(tcp_sockets[fd].snd_ssthresh & 0xff)
        reply[1 + 77] = byte((tcp_sockets[fd].snd_ssthresh >> 8) & 0xff)
        reply[1 + 78] = byte((tcp_sockets[fd].snd_ssthresh >> 16) & 0xff)
        reply[1 + 79] = byte((tcp_sockets[fd].snd_ssthresh >> 24) & 0xff)
        reply[1 + 80] = byte(tcp_sockets[fd].snd_cwnd & 0xff)
        reply[1 + 81] = byte((tcp_sockets[fd].snd_cwnd >> 8) & 0xff)
        reply[1 + 82] = byte((tcp_sockets[fd].snd_cwnd >> 16) & 0xff)
        reply[1 + 83] = byte((tcp_sockets[fd].snd_cwnd >> 24) & 0xff)
        // tcpi_advmss @ 84 — same as snd_mss for us.
        reply[1 + 84] = byte(smss & 0xff)
        reply[1 + 85] = byte((smss >> 8) & 0xff)
        reply[1 + 86] = byte((smss >> 16) & 0xff)
        reply[1 + 87] = byte((smss >> 24) & 0xff)
        // tcpi_total_retrans @ 100
        reply[1 + 100] = byte(tcp_sockets[fd].rexmit_count & 0xff)
        reply[1 + 101] = byte((tcp_sockets[fd].rexmit_count >> 8) & 0xff)
        reply[1 + 102] = byte((tcp_sockets[fd].rexmit_count >> 16) & 0xff)
        reply[1 + 103] = byte((tcp_sockets[fd].rexmit_count >> 24) & 0xff)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 105)


    inet_handle_tcp_inject_fail(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val rport = inet_read_u16(buf, 1) & 0xffff
        var reply: [1]byte
        var i = 0
        var hit = -1
        while i < TCP_MAX_SOCKETS
            if tcp_sockets[i].in_use != 0
                if tcp_sockets[i].state == TCP_STATE_SYN_SENT
                    if tcp_sockets[i].remote_port == rport
                        hit = i
                        i = TCP_MAX_SOCKETS
            i += 1
        if hit < 0
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)
            return
        tcp_sockets[hit].pending_error = 111  // ECONNREFUSED
        inet_tcp_close_slot(hit, -1)
        reply[0] = byte(0)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)


SO_LINGER `l_linger = 0` close path.  Shim sends this when the
caller has set linger-on with a zero timeout: emit RST, free
slot, no FIN, no TIME_WAIT.  Reply is immediate (1-byte status)
since no FIN handshake happens.

    inet_handle_tcp_abort_close(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val fd = inet_read_u16(buf, 1) & 0xffff
        var reply: [1]byte
        if inet_tcp_slot_owned(fd, sender) == 0
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)
            return
        val rc = inet_tcp_abort(fd)
        reply[0] = byte(rc & 0xff)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)

    inet_handle_tcp_listen(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val port = inet_read_u16(buf, 1)
        val backlog = inet_read_u16(buf, 3) & 0xffff
        val sndbuf = inet_read_u16(buf, 5)
        val rcvbuf = inet_read_u16(buf, 7)
        val s = inet_tcp_begin_listen(sender, port, backlog, sndbuf, rcvbuf)
        var reply: [2]byte
        if s < 0
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
            reply[1] = 0
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 2)
            return
        reply[0] = 0
        reply[1] = byte(s & 0xff)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 2)

    inet_handle_tcp_accept(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val fd = inet_read_u16(buf, 1) & 0xffff
        if inet_tcp_slot_owned(fd, sender) == 0
            var err: [8]byte
            err[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 8)
            return
        val child = inet_tcp_do_accept(sender, fd)
        if child == -2
            var err: [8]byte
            err[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 8)
            return
        if child >= 0
            inet_tcp_reply_accept(sender, 0, child)
            return
        // child == -1 → caller parked; state_step will reply once a
        // SYN_RCVD child reaches ESTABLISHED.

Non-blocking accept. Identical wire to `INET_CMD_TCP_ACCEPT` but
never parks the caller — replies -EAGAIN if the queue is empty
so an event-loop client can drive accept() entirely from epoll
notifications. The shim picks this variant when the listen fd
has `O_NONBLOCK` set.

    inet_handle_tcp_accept_nb(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val fd = inet_read_u16(buf, 1) & 0xffff
        if inet_tcp_slot_owned(fd, sender) == 0
            var err: [8]byte
            err[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 8)
            return
        val child = inet_tcp_try_accept(fd)
        if child == -2
            var err: [8]byte
            err[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 8)
            return
        if child >= 0
            inet_tcp_reply_accept(sender, 0, child)
            return
        // Queue empty — return -EAGAIN without parking.
        var eagain: [8]byte
        eagain[0] = byte(-11)
        ipc_reply(sender, eagain, 8)


### inet_handle_set_ip_config

Install a new (ip, mask, gw) triple via `inet_set_ip_config`. Used
by the DHCP client once a lease is bound, and exposed as an IPC op
so any userspace tool (ifconfig, tests) can drive it. Wire format:
cmd(1) + ip(4) + mask(4) + gw(4) = 13 bytes in; 1 byte status out.

    inet_handle_set_ip_config(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val ip = inet_read_ipv4(buf, 1)
        val mask = inet_read_ipv4(buf, 5)
        val gw = inet_read_ipv4(buf, 9)
        inet_set_ip_config(ip, mask, gw)
        var reply: [1]byte
        reply[0] = byte(0)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)


### inet_handle_pid_exit

Reclaim every socket owned by a terminated thread. PM sends the
dead process's main_tid as a big-endian i32 at buf[1..5]; inet
scans both the UDP and TCP tables and closes any socket whose
`owner_tid` matches. This is how we prevent inet_sockets[] /
tcp_sockets[] slots from leaking when a process exits without
calling close(). Wire format: cmd(1) + tid(4) = 5 in, status(1)
out. Cleanup is ungraceful — TCP sockets skip FIN and go straight
to CLOSED, which is fine for the pid-exit case (the process is
gone and the peer will observe connection loss via timeout or a
future RST).

    inet_handle_pid_exit(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val tid = ((int(buf[1]) & 0xff) << 24) | ((int(buf[2]) & 0xff) << 16) | ((int(buf[3]) & 0xff) << 8) | (int(buf[4]) & 0xff)
        var i = 0
        while i < INET_MAX_SOCKETS
            if inet_sockets[i].in_use != 0
                if inet_sockets[i].owner_tid == tid
                    if inet_sockets[i].bound != 0
                        inet_udp_hash_remove(i)
                    inet_sockets[i].in_use = 0
                    inet_sockets[i].bound = 0
                    inet_sockets[i].q_count = 0
                    inet_sockets[i].q_head = 0
                    inet_sockets[i].q_tail = 0
                    inet_sockets[i].pending_tid = -1
                    inet_sockets[i].recv_deadline = 0
            i += 1
        inet_tcp_cleanup_owner(tid)
        inet_epoll_drop_owner(tid)
        // Phase 2 chunk 3 — auxiliary tables that hold owner_tid:
        // ICMP ping pending replies (4 slots, easy to fill) and
        // ARP-pending UDP datagrams (2 slots, ditto).  Without this
        // a process that pings an unresponsive peer or sends to an
        // unresolved next-hop and then dies leaks the slot until
        // an unrelated id+seq collision or ARP cache eviction frees
        // it.
        inet_ping_cleanup_owner(tid)
        inet_pending_udp_cleanup_owner(tid)
        var reply: [1]byte
        reply[0] = byte(0)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)


### inet_handle_udp_getname

Return the local endpoint of a UDP socket: (port, ip). Used by
`getsockname` from the POSIX shim. UDP has no persistent peer, so
`getpeername` on UDP short-circuits in the shim and this command
never fires with a "peer" variant — hence a single op with no
which-flag. Wire: cmd(1) + fd(u8) = 2 bytes in. Reply: status(1)
+ port(2 BE) + ip(4) = 7 bytes. An unbound socket reports
`0.0.0.0:0`.

    inet_handle_udp_getname(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val fd = int(buf[1]) & 0xff
        var reply: [7]byte
        if inet_fd_valid(fd, sender) == 0
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
            inet_write_u16(reply, 1, 0)
            inet_write_ipv4(reply, 3, u32(0))
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 7)
            return
        reply[0] = byte(0)
        if inet_sockets[fd].bound != 0
            inet_write_u16(reply, 1, inet_sockets[fd].bound_port)
            inet_write_ipv4(reply, 3, inet_sockets[fd].bound_ip)
        else
            inet_write_u16(reply, 1, 0)
            inet_write_ipv4(reply, 3, u32(0))
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 7)


### inet_handle_tcp_getname

Return either the local or peer endpoint of a TCP socket. Wire:
cmd(1) + fd(u16) + which(u8) = 4 bytes in; which == 0 means
local, which == 1 means peer. Reply: status(1) + port(2 BE) +
ip(4) = 7 bytes. Returns `-ENOTCONN` (-107) for a peer query on
a socket that hasn't reached ESTABLISHED. The heavy lifting lives
in `inet_tcp_getname` (inet_proto.lsysl) because it touches
`tcp_sockets[]` state.

    inet_handle_tcp_getname(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val fd = inet_read_u16(buf, 1) & 0xffff
        val which = int(buf[3]) & 0xff
        var reply: [7]byte
        if inet_tcp_slot_owned(fd, sender) == 0
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
            inet_write_u16(reply, 1, 0)
            inet_write_ipv4(reply, 3, u32(0))
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 7)
            return
        var port = 0
        var ip = u32(0)
        val status = inet_tcp_getname(fd, which, &port, &ip)
        reply[0] = byte(status)
        inet_write_u16(reply, 1, port)
        inet_write_ipv4(reply, 3, ip)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 7)


### inet_handle_tcp_setbuf / inet_handle_tcp_getbuf

setsockopt(SO_SNDBUF) / setsockopt(SO_RCVBUF). The shim translates
the user `int optval` into a 16-bit BE size on the wire (clamped on
the inet side to [TCP_BUF_MIN, TCP_BUF_MAX]). `kind == 0` selects
SNDBUF, `kind == 1` selects RCVBUF.

SET wire: cmd(1) + fd(u16 BE) + kind(1) + size(u16 BE) = 6 bytes in;
reply is 1 byte status (0 = ok, -1 = bad fd / bad kind).

GET wire: cmd(1) + fd(u16 BE) + kind(1) = 4 bytes in; reply is
1 byte status + u16 BE size = 3 bytes out (size 0 on error).

    inet_handle_tcp_setbuf(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val fd = inet_read_u16(buf, 1) & 0xffff
        val kind = int(buf[3]) & 0xff
        val size = inet_read_u16(buf, 4)
        var reply: [1]byte
        if inet_tcp_slot_owned(fd, sender) == 0
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)
            return
        val rc = inet_tcp_resize_buf(fd, kind, size)
        reply[0] = byte(rc & 0xff)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)

    inet_handle_tcp_getbuf(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val fd = inet_read_u16(buf, 1) & 0xffff
        val kind = int(buf[3]) & 0xff
        var reply: [3]byte
        if inet_tcp_slot_owned(fd, sender) == 0
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
            inet_write_u16(reply, 1, 0)
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 3)
            return
        val sz = inet_tcp_get_bufsize(fd, kind)
        if sz < 0
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
            inet_write_u16(reply, 1, 0)
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 3)
            return
        reply[0] = 0
        inet_write_u16(reply, 1, sz)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 3)


### inet_handle_get_ip_config

Return the current (ip, mask, gw). Reply: status(1) + ip(4) +
mask(4) + gw(4) = 13 bytes. Payload-less request (just cmd).

    inet_handle_get_ip_config(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        var reply: [13]byte
        reply[0] = byte(0)
        inet_write_ipv4(reply, 1, inet_get_our_ip())
        inet_write_ipv4(reply, 5, inet_get_mask())
        inet_write_ipv4(reply, 9, inet_get_gw())
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 13)


Test-only: drive inet_tcp_state_step through a deliberately out-of-
order segment sequence against a scratch socket and verify the
reorder queue yields the right rcv_nxt / recv_buf contents. Reply
is 1 byte: 0 on pass, nonzero error code on failure.

    inet_handle_tcp_ooo_selftest(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val rc = inet_tcp_ooo_selftest()
        var reply: [1]byte
        reply[0] = byte(rc & 0xff)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)


Test-only: drive `inet_tcp_send_park_selftest` to verify the
park/wake state machine added in Phase 1 — fill send_buf, park a
fake waiter, drain, watch the helper clear the slot.

    inet_handle_tcp_send_park_test(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val rc = inet_tcp_send_park_selftest()
        var reply: [1]byte
        reply[0] = byte(rc & 0xff)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)


Test-only: drive `inet_tcp_arp_replay_selftest` to verify that
landing an ARP reply re-emits any TCP socket that previously
hit a MAC-miss in `inet_tcp_emit`.

    inet_handle_tcp_arp_replay_test(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val rc = inet_tcp_arp_replay_selftest()
        var reply: [1]byte
        reply[0] = byte(rc & 0xff)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)


Test-only: drive the Phase 2 chunk 3 PID_EXIT cleanup audit.
Synthesizes a fake owner_tid (-7777, well outside the real tid
space), parks one ICMP ping slot and one pending-UDP slot under
that tid, runs the cleanup helpers, and verifies both slots
freed.  Reply byte breaks down as:
  0          all clear
  0x10..0x1f ping selftest failed (low 4 bits = step)
  0x20..0x2f pending-UDP selftest failed (low 4 bits = step)

    inet_handle_pid_exit_selftest(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val fake_tid = -7777
        val pr = inet_ping_cleanup_selftest(fake_tid)
        var reply: [1]byte
        if pr != 0
            reply[0] = byte(0x10 | (pr & 0x0f))
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)
            return
        val ur = inet_pending_udp_cleanup_selftest(fake_tid)
        if ur != 0
            reply[0] = byte(0x20 | (ur & 0x0f))
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)
            return
        reply[0] = byte(0)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)


### inet_handle_get_stats

Phase 2 chunk 4 — caller-side introspection of net-stack
resource use. Lets a process answer "how many sockets and how
much TCP buffer memory am I currently holding?" without parsing
`/proc/net/*` style files. Closes Phase 2 of the netstack-quality
plan.

Wire:

  request:  cmd(1) + tid_be32(4) = 5 bytes.  tid==0 means
            "use the sender's tid"; nonzero is an explicit query
            that lets a parent inspect a child or a test inspect
            a synthetic owner.

  reply:    status(1) + tcp_count_be32(4) + udp_count_be32(4)
            + tcp_buf_bytes_be32(4) + reserved_be32(4) = 17 bytes.
            status==0 on success, 0xff on error (currently the
            handler can't fail — slot scans never error — but we
            keep the byte for symmetry with the rest of the IPC
            ABI and to leave room for ACL gating later).

The three counts are computed by the existing per-owner helpers
(`inet_count_tcp_owner`, `inet_count_udp_owner`,
`inet_tcp_buf_bytes_owner`), so this handler is essentially three
calls plus packing.

    inet_handle_get_stats(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val t0 = (int(buf[1]) & 0xff) << 24
        val t1 = (int(buf[2]) & 0xff) << 16
        val t2 = (int(buf[3]) & 0xff) <<  8
        val t3 =  int(buf[4]) & 0xff
        val tid_arg = t0 | t1 | t2 | t3
        var whose = tid_arg
        if tid_arg == 0
            whose = sender
        val tcp_n = inet_count_tcp_owner(whose)
        val udp_n = inet_count_udp_owner(whose)
        val buf_b = inet_tcp_buf_bytes_owner(whose)
        var reply: [17]byte
        reply[0] = byte(0)
        reply[1] = byte((tcp_n >> 24) & 0xff)
        reply[2] = byte((tcp_n >> 16) & 0xff)
        reply[3] = byte((tcp_n >>  8) & 0xff)
        reply[4] = byte( tcp_n        & 0xff)
        reply[5] = byte((udp_n >> 24) & 0xff)
        reply[6] = byte((udp_n >> 16) & 0xff)
        reply[7] = byte((udp_n >>  8) & 0xff)
        reply[8] = byte( udp_n        & 0xff)
        reply[ 9] = byte((buf_b >> 24) & 0xff)
        reply[10] = byte((buf_b >> 16) & 0xff)
        reply[11] = byte((buf_b >>  8) & 0xff)
        reply[12] = byte( buf_b        & 0xff)
        reply[13] = byte(0)
        reply[14] = byte(0)
        reply[15] = byte(0)
        reply[16] = byte(0)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 17)


`inet_handle_frame_pool_stats` lives in `inet_proto.lsysl` next to
the pool storage. Dispatch wires it from this file via a forward
call.


Test-only: synthesize three IPv4 fragments of a 32-byte UDP
datagram bound for our IP at port 9100, inject them into
`inet_handle_frame` in out-of-order sequence (frag 2, frag 3,
frag 1), and reply 1 byte status (0 on success, nonzero error).
The assembled UDP payload reaches the test program through the
normal RX path — when it calls `recvfrom` it picks up the
reassembled datagram from its bound socket's queue.

UDP layout (post-assembly, IP payload bytes 0..32):
  byte  0..2  src port = 9000
  byte  2..4  dst port = 9100
  byte  4..6  udp length = 32
  byte  6..8  udp checksum = 0 (skip verify)
  byte  8..16 'A' x 8
  byte 16..24 'B' x 8
  byte 24..32 'C' x 8

Fragment plan:
  frag 1: offset=0,  MF=1, body=ud[0..16]   (UDP header + 'A'x8)
  frag 2: offset=16, MF=1, body=ud[16..24]  ('B' x 8)
  frag 3: offset=24, MF=0, body=ud[24..32]  ('C' x 8)

    inet_handle_ip_reasm_selftest(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val rc = inet_ip_reasm_selftest_run()
        var reply: [1]byte
        reply[0] = byte(rc & 0xff)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)

    inet_ip_reasm_selftest_run() -> int
        var ud: [32]byte
        inet_write_u16(ud, 0, 9000)
        inet_write_u16(ud, 2, 9100)
        inet_write_u16(ud, 4, 32)
        inet_write_u16(ud, 6, 0)
        var bi = 0
        while bi < 8
            ud[8 + bi] = byte('A')
            ud[16 + bi] = byte('B')
            ud[24 + bi] = byte('C')
            bi += 1

        if inet_ip_reasm_inject_frag(0xABCD, 16, 1, ud, 16, 8) != 0
            return 1
        if inet_ip_reasm_inject_frag(0xABCD, 24, 0, ud, 24, 8) != 0
            return 2
        if inet_ip_reasm_inject_frag(0xABCD, 0, 1, ud, 0, 16) != 0
            return 3
        0

Build a single IPv4 fragment frame and feed it through
`inet_handle_frame`. ETH MACs are zeroed — handle_frame doesn't
inspect ETH dst once we're past the NIC.

    inet_ip_reasm_inject_frag(ident_v: int, offset_bytes: int, mf: int,
                              src: *byte, src_off: int, body_len: int) -> int
        var frame: [80]byte
        var i = 0
        while i < 14
            frame[i] = byte(0)
            i += 1
        inet_write_u16(frame, 12, ETHERTYPE_IPV4)

        frame[14] = byte(0x45)
        frame[15] = byte(0)
        inet_write_u16(frame, 16, 20 + body_len)
        inet_write_u16(frame, 18, ident_v)
        val flags_v = (mf << 13) | (offset_bytes / 8)
        inet_write_u16(frame, 20, flags_v)
        frame[22] = byte(64)
        frame[23] = byte(IP_PROTO_UDP)
        inet_write_u16(frame, 24, 0)
        inet_write_ipv4(frame, 26, ipv4(10, 0, 2, 99))
        inet_write_ipv4(frame, 30, inet_get_our_ip())
        inet_write_u16(frame, 24, net_checksum(frame + 14, 20))

        var k = 0
        while k < body_len
            frame[14 + 20 + k] = src[src_off + k]
            k += 1

        inet_handle_frame(frame, 14 + 20 + body_len)
        0


Test-only: RFC 5722 overlap-fragment enforcement. Inject a
first-fragment ('X' x 16) at offset=0, then an overlapping
fragment ('Y' x 8) at offset=8 — overlapping bytes 8..16 of the
existing range. The reassembly slot must be poisoned in
response. Reply 1 byte: 0 on pass, nonzero on failure.

    inet_handle_ip_reasm_overlap_test(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val rc = inet_ip_reasm_overlap_test_run()
        var reply: [1]byte
        reply[0] = byte(rc & 0xff)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)

    inet_ip_reasm_overlap_test_run() -> int
        val id_v = 0xCAFE
        var ud: [24]byte
        var bi = 0
        while bi < 16
            ud[bi] = byte('X')
            bi += 1
        bi = 0
        while bi < 8
            ud[16 + bi] = byte('Y')
            bi += 1
        if inet_ip_reasm_inject_frag(id_v, 0, 1, ud, 0, 16) != 0
            return 1
        if inet_ip_reasm_inject_frag(id_v, 8, 1, ud, 16, 8) != 0
            return 2
        val s = inet_ip_reasm_find(ipv4(10, 0, 2, 99), inet_get_our_ip(),
                                   id_v, IP_PROTO_UDP)
        if s < 0
            return 3
        val was_poisoned = inet_ip_reasm_table[s].poisoned
        inet_ip_reasm_force_expire(s)
        inet_ip_reasm_scan_timeouts()
        if was_poisoned == 0
            return 4
        0


Test-only: ICMP Time Exceeded emit on reassembly timeout. Inject
one first-fragment, force its deadline past, run the scan sweep,
and verify `inet_ip_reasm_timeouts_sent` advanced by exactly one.
Reply 1 byte: 0 on pass, nonzero on failure.

    inet_handle_ip_reasm_timeout_test(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val rc = inet_ip_reasm_timeout_test_run()
        var reply: [1]byte
        reply[0] = byte(rc & 0xff)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)

    inet_ip_reasm_timeout_test_run() -> int
        val before = inet_ip_reasm_timeouts_sent
        val id_v = 0xBABE
        var ud: [16]byte
        var i = 0
        while i < 16
            ud[i] = byte('Q')
            i += 1
        if inet_ip_reasm_inject_frag(id_v, 0, 1, ud, 0, 16) != 0
            return 1
        val s = inet_ip_reasm_find(ipv4(10, 0, 2, 99), inet_get_our_ip(),
                                   id_v, IP_PROTO_UDP)
        if s < 0
            return 2
        inet_ip_reasm_force_expire(s)
        inet_ip_reasm_scan_timeouts()
        val after = inet_ip_reasm_timeouts_sent
        if after - before != 1
            return 3
        if inet_ip_reasm_table[s].in_use != 0
            return 4
        0


### UDP — inbound

Dispatch an IPv4/UDP frame that already passed the destination-IP
check. Pulls header fields, looks up a socket bound to `dst_port`,
and either hands the datagram to a waiting recvfrom or enqueues it.
Dropping on no-match matches standard UDP semantics.

    inet_handle_udp(frame: *byte, total_len: int, ihl_bytes: int)
        val udp_off = 14 + ihl_bytes
        if total_len < udp_off + 8
            return
        val src_port = pkt_read_u16(frame, udp_off)
        val dst_port = pkt_read_u16(frame, udp_off + 2)
        val udp_len = pkt_read_u16(frame, udp_off + 4)
        if udp_len < 8
            return
        if total_len < udp_off + udp_len
            return
        val payload_len = udp_len - 8
        val data: *byte = frame + udp_off + 8
        val src_ip = ipv4(int(frame[14 + 12]) & 0xff, int(frame[14 + 13]) & 0xff, int(frame[14 + 14]) & 0xff, int(frame[14 + 15]) & 0xff)
        // Read dst from the wire rather than hardcoding our own IP:
        // broadcast UDP (DHCP OFFER/ACK) arrives with dst=255.255.255.255
        // and the checksum must pseudo-header it accordingly.
        val dst_ip = ipv4(int(frame[14 + 16]) & 0xff, int(frame[14 + 17]) & 0xff, int(frame[14 + 18]) & 0xff, int(frame[14 + 19]) & 0xff)
        if inet_udp_verify(src_ip, dst_ip, frame, udp_off, udp_len) == 0
            return
        val target = inet_find_bound_port(dst_port)
        if target < 0
            // No socket bound — emit ICMP Port Unreachable so the
            // peer's connect()ed UDP fd can return -ECONNREFUSED
            // immediately instead of timing out (Linux semantics).
            // Skip when the destination wasn't unicast to us — RFC
            // 1812 §4.3.2.7 forbids ICMP errors for broadcast /
            // multicast / 0.0.0.0 destinations to prevent
            // amplification.  Slix only owns one IP, so we check
            // dst_ip against ours and bail otherwise.
            if pkt_match_ipv4(frame, 14 + 16, INET_OUR_IP_A,
                              INET_OUR_IP_B, INET_OUR_IP_C,
                              INET_OUR_IP_D) != 0
                inet_send_icmp_port_unreach(frame, total_len)
            return
        if inet_sockets[target].pending_tid >= 0
            inet_deliver_to_waiter(target, src_ip, src_port, data, payload_len)
        else
            inet_enqueue(target, src_ip, src_port, data, payload_len)


### Inbound ICMP Destination Unreachable → connected UDP socket

Pairs with `inet_send_icmp_port_unreach`: when a peer reports our
UDP datagram couldn't be delivered, surface the error on the
socket that originated the send. RFC 792 §3.4 puts the offending
frame's IP header + first 8 bytes of L4 in the ICMP body, so we
parse the inner UDP header to find our local source port and look
the socket up by it.

We only handle UDP for now — the inner-protocol check skips TCP
and others. TCP error consumption (RST-on-ICMP) is its own piece
of work; the UDP path is what unblocks the typical
`connect()` / `sendto()` / `recvfrom()` loop returning
`-ECONNREFUSED` instead of timing out.

We don't validate the inner peer address against any
connect()ed peer — Linux does, but slix's UDP is permissive
enough that local-port match is good enough. A pathological
program with multiple sendto destinations from the same local
port may flag the wrong one, but that's bounded scope.

    val ICMP_ERRNO_ECONNREFUSED = 111

    inet_handle_icmp_dest_unreach(frame: *byte, len: int, icmp_off: int)
        val body_off = icmp_off + 8
        if len < body_off + 20
            return
        val inner_ihl = (int(frame[body_off]) & 0x0f) * 4
        if inner_ihl < 20
            return
        val inner_proto = int(frame[body_off + 9]) & 0xff
        if inner_proto != IP_PROTO_UDP
            return
        val inner_udp_off = body_off + inner_ihl
        if len < inner_udp_off + 8
            return
        val inner_src_port = pkt_read_u16(frame, inner_udp_off + 0)
        val target = inet_find_bound_port(inner_src_port)
        if target < 0
            return
        inet_sockets[target].pending_error = ICMP_ERRNO_ECONNREFUSED
        // If a recvfrom is parked on this socket, deliver the error
        // immediately so the caller wakes up; otherwise the next
        // recvfrom will pick the error up from pending_error.
        if inet_sockets[target].pending_tid >= 0
            val waiter = inet_sockets[target].pending_tid
            inet_sockets[target].pending_tid = -1
            inet_sockets[target].recv_deadline = 0
            inet_sockets[target].pending_error = 0
            var er: [10]byte
            er[0] = byte(2)
            ipc_reply(waiter, er, 10)
        // Wake any epoll waiter so a poll loop notices via EPOLLERR
        // (the user will then call recvfrom and get -ECONNREFUSED).
        inet_epoll_fire(1, target)


### Synthetic ICMP-error injection (test-only)

Drives `inet_handle_icmp_dest_unreach` from a test program without
needing a real on-the-wire ICMP error. Caller passes the local
UDP source port that originally sent the (fictitious) datagram;
we synthesize the minimal RFC 792 "outer ICMP + inner IP+UDP"
frame with that port at the inner UDP src offset, and feed it
through the real parser.

Wire layout:
- request: cmd(1) + local_port(2)         = 3 bytes
- reply:   status(1)                       = 1 byte (0 always; the
  parser is fire-and-forget — failures show up as -ECONNREFUSED
  not arriving on recvfrom)

    inet_handle_icmp_unreach_inject(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val local_port = inet_read_u16(buf, 1)
        // Layout (offset / size):
        //   0  / 14  ETH placeholder
        //  14 / 20  IPv4 outer (only IHL nibble matters)
        //  34 / 8   ICMP outer (type+code rest unused)
        //  42 / 20  inner IPv4 (proto field at +9 must be UDP)
        //  62 / 8   inner UDP   (src_port at +0 = our local port)
        // = 70 bytes total.
        var frame: [70]byte
        var k = 0
        while k < 70
            frame[k] = byte(0)
            k += 1
        frame[14] = byte(0x45)               // ver=4, IHL=5
        frame[34] = byte(ICMP_DEST_UNREACH)
        frame[35] = byte(3)                  // PORT_UNREACH
        frame[42] = byte(0x45)               // inner ver=4, IHL=5
        frame[42 + 9] = byte(IP_PROTO_UDP)   // inner protocol
        frame[42 + 20 + 0] = byte((local_port >> 8) & 0xff)
        frame[42 + 20 + 1] = byte(local_port & 0xff)
        // icmp_off = 14 + 20 = 34
        inet_handle_icmp_dest_unreach(frame, 70, 34)
        var reply: [1]byte
        reply[0] = 0
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)


### EtherType dispatch

Called by the RX drain for every frame. ARP requests for us ->
reply, ARP reply from gateway -> cache gw MAC + send one ICMP
echo, ICMP echo request -> reply, ICMP echo reply -> diag count,
UDP -> socket dispatch. Returns 1 if we acted, 0 otherwise
(diagnostic only).

    inet_handle_frame(frame: *byte, len: int) -> int
        if len < 14
            return 0
        val etype = pkt_read_u16(frame, 12)
        if etype == ETHERTYPE_ARP
            if len < 14 + 28
                return 0
            val op = pkt_read_u16(frame, 14 + 6)
            // ARP probe conflict detection (RFC 5227 §2.1.1).
            // Every ARP frame whose sender IP matches the address
            // being probed counts as a defender — *unless* its
            // sender MAC matches ours, in which case it's our own
            // probe looped back through the NIC. We watch both
            // REQUEST and REPLY since a defender can use either.
            if inet_arp_probe_matches(frame) != 0
                arp_probe_seen = 1
            if op == ARP_OP_REQUEST
                if pkt_match_ipv4(frame, 14 + 24, INET_OUR_IP_A, INET_OUR_IP_B, INET_OUR_IP_C, INET_OUR_IP_D) == 0
                    return 0
                inet_send_arp_reply(frame)
                return 1
            if op == ARP_OP_REPLY
                // Cache (sender IP, sender MAC) for any reply — the
                // payer stack wants to know anyone who answered, not
                // just the gateway. ARP frame layout:
                //   14..19 HTYPE/PTYPE/HLEN/PLEN
                //   20..21 OPER
                //   22..27 SHA (sender MAC)   <- frame + 14 + 8
                //   28..31 SPA (sender IPv4)  <- frame + 14 + 14
                val sender_ip = ipv4(
                    int(frame[14 + 14]) & 0xff,
                    int(frame[14 + 15]) & 0xff,
                    int(frame[14 + 16]) & 0xff,
                    int(frame[14 + 17]) & 0xff)
                inet_arp_insert(sender_ip, frame + 14 + 8)
                // Keep the boot-demo ICMP echo on the first gateway
                // reply so the existing pcap regression still shows
                // the full ARP → ICMP round-trip.
                if pkt_match_ipv4(frame, 14 + 14, INET_GW_IP_A, INET_GW_IP_B, INET_GW_IP_C, INET_GW_IP_D) != 0
                    inet_send_icmp_echo(0x1234, 1)
                return 1
            return 0
        if etype == ETHERTYPE_IPV4
            if len < 14 + 20
                return 0
            val ihl_bytes = (int(frame[14]) & 0x0f) * 4
            if ihl_bytes < 20
                return 0
            // Accept unicast to us OR the limited-broadcast
            // 255.255.255.255. The broadcast allowance lets DHCP
            // OFFER/ACK through pre-lease (we have no IP yet at
            // DISCOVER time, so the server replies by broadcast
            // per RFC 2131 §4.1).
            val is_unicast = pkt_match_ipv4(frame, 14 + 16, INET_OUR_IP_A, INET_OUR_IP_B, INET_OUR_IP_C, INET_OUR_IP_D)
            val is_bcast = pkt_match_ipv4(frame, 14 + 16, 255, 255, 255, 255)
            if is_unicast == 0
                if is_bcast == 0
                    return 0
            // IP fragment? bits 12..0 of frame[14+6..14+8] are MF +
            // fragment offset. Anything nonzero means this is part of
            // a fragmented datagram and the L4 header isn't where the
            // proto switch would expect it. Hand off to reassembly;
            // when the final fragment arrives the slot synthesizes a
            // contiguous frame and we re-enter on it.
            val flags_frag = pkt_read_u16(frame, 14 + 6)
            if (flags_frag & 0x3FFF) != 0
                val ip_total_in = pkt_read_u16(frame, 14 + 2)
                val payload_len = ip_total_in - ihl_bytes
                if payload_len <= 0
                    return 0
                val ident_v = pkt_read_u16(frame, 14 + 4)
                val mf_v = (flags_frag >> 13) & 1
                val offset_v = (flags_frag & 0x1FFF) * 8
                val frag_proto = int(frame[14 + 9]) & 0xff
                val frag_src = ipv4(
                    int(frame[14 + 12]) & 0xff, int(frame[14 + 13]) & 0xff,
                    int(frame[14 + 14]) & 0xff, int(frame[14 + 15]) & 0xff)
                val frag_dst = ipv4(
                    int(frame[14 + 16]) & 0xff, int(frame[14 + 17]) & 0xff,
                    int(frame[14 + 18]) & 0xff, int(frame[14 + 19]) & 0xff)
                val flen = inet_ip_reasm_accept(frame, frag_src, frag_dst,
                    ident_v, frag_proto, mf_v, offset_v, ihl_bytes,
                    frame + 14 + ihl_bytes, payload_len)
                if flen > 0
                    inet_handle_frame(inet_ip_reasm_staging, flen)
                return 1
            val proto = int(frame[14 + 9]) & 0xff
            if proto == IP_PROTO_ICMP
                val icmp_off = 14 + ihl_bytes
                if len < icmp_off + 8
                    return 0
                val itype = int(frame[icmp_off]) & 0xff
                if itype == ICMP_ECHO_REQ
                    inet_send_icmp_reply(frame, len)
                    return 1
                if itype == ICMP_ECHO_REP
                    inet_bump_pings_received()
                    val rep_id = pkt_read_u16(frame, icmp_off + 4)
                    val rep_seq = pkt_read_u16(frame, icmp_off + 6)
                    val rep_src = ipv4(
                        int(frame[14 + 12]) & 0xff,
                        int(frame[14 + 13]) & 0xff,
                        int(frame[14 + 14]) & 0xff,
                        int(frame[14 + 15]) & 0xff)
                    inet_ping_deliver(rep_src, rep_id, rep_seq, int(uptime()))
                    return 1
                if itype == ICMP_DEST_UNREACH
                    inet_handle_icmp_dest_unreach(frame, len, icmp_off)
                    return 1
                return 0
            if proto == IP_PROTO_UDP
                inet_handle_udp(frame, len, ihl_bytes)
                return 1
            if proto == IP_PROTO_TCP
                inet_tcp_handle_segment(frame, len, ihl_bytes)
                return 1
            return 0
        0


### RX drain

Called on every `ipc_recv_notify` wake where the result was -2
(a nic notification). Drains the RX queue non-blockingly, feeding
each frame into `inet_handle_frame`.

    inet_drain_rx()
        val idx = inet_frame_alloc()
        val frame = inet_frame_buf(idx)
        while true
            val n = inet_nic_recv_packet(frame, 1514)
            if n <= 0
                break
            inet_handle_frame(frame, n)
        inet_frame_release(idx)


### Link-layer init

Called once from `inet_server()` after it registers its port.
Fetches our MAC from the nic, subscribes for RX notifications,
and fires the boot ARP request for the gateway (so the familiar
ARP + ICMP echo pcap still drops out of a cold boot).

Returns 1 if the nic responded and we subscribed successfully, 0
if the nic isn't reachable (e.g. x86 builds without virtio-net).
UDP loopback works either way.

    inet_link_init() -> int
        var mac: [6]byte
        if inet_nic_get_mac(mac) != 0
            return 0
        inet_set_our_mac(mac)
        if inet_nic_subscribe_rx() != 0
            return 0
        inet_send_arp_request(INET_GW_IP_A, INET_GW_IP_B, INET_GW_IP_C, INET_GW_IP_D)
        1


### inet_handle_epoll_sub / unsub / inst_close

Wire format for SUB/UNSUB: cmd(1) + kind(1) + fd(2 BE) +
epoll_inst(1) = 5 bytes in. INST_CLOSE: cmd(1) + epoll_inst(1)
= 2 bytes in. Reply is always status(1).

SUB upserts — if the same (kind, fd, owner_tid, epoll_inst)
quad is already registered, it's a no-op success. The shim
calls SUB on every CTL_ADD and CTL_MOD; the `events` mask isn't
shipped because inet's job is just "wake on edge", not filtering.

    inet_handle_epoll_sub(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val kind = int(buf[1]) & 0xff
        val fd = inet_read_u16(buf, 2) & 0xffff
        val inst = int(buf[4]) & 0xff
        var reply: [1]byte
        val existing = inet_epoll_find_sub(kind, fd, sender, inst)
        if existing >= 0
            reply[0] = byte(0)
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)
            return
        val slot = inet_epoll_alloc_sub()
        if slot < 0
            reply[0] = byte(-28)            // -ENOSPC
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)
            return
        inet_epoll_subs[slot].in_use = 1
        inet_epoll_subs[slot].kind = kind
        inet_epoll_subs[slot].inet_fd = fd
        inet_epoll_subs[slot].owner_tid = sender
        inet_epoll_subs[slot].epoll_inst = inst
        reply[0] = byte(0)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)

    inet_handle_epoll_unsub(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val kind = int(buf[1]) & 0xff
        val fd = inet_read_u16(buf, 2) & 0xffff
        val inst = int(buf[4]) & 0xff
        var reply: [1]byte
        val slot = inet_epoll_find_sub(kind, fd, sender, inst)
        if slot >= 0
            inet_epoll_subs[slot].in_use = 0
        reply[0] = byte(0)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)

    inet_handle_epoll_inst_close(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val inst = int(buf[1]) & 0xff
        inet_epoll_drop_inst(sender, inst)
        var reply: [1]byte
        reply[0] = byte(0)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)


### Server main loop

Uses `ipc_recv_notify` so the same loop wakes on both socket IPC
(SOCKET/BIND/SENDTO/...) and async nic RX notifications. On a
notification we drain the nic's RX queue via
`inet_nic_recv_packet` and dispatch each frame through
`inet_handle_frame` (ARP + ICMP echo).

Before the loop, `inet_link_init()` grabs our MAC from the nic,
subscribes for RX notifications, and fires the boot ARP request
for the gateway. It's a best-effort call — if the nic isn't
reachable (e.g. the x86 build, which has no virtio-net yet) we
silently skip link-layer setup and still serve UDP loopback.

    inet_server()
        inet_init_sockets()
        inet_tcp_init()
        inet_epoll_init()
        inet_port = port_create()
        port_register(inet_port, "inet")
        rs_ready()

        inet_link_init()

        // Periodic wakeup for TCP retransmit / TIME_WAIT scans.
        // 5 ticks ≈ 50 ms on a 100 Hz scheduler timer.
        svc_timer_subscribe(5)

        // Borrow a pool slot for the IPC receive buffer; held for
        // the server's entire lifetime so subsequent allocs see
        // baseline `used == 1`.
        val main_idx = inet_frame_alloc()
        val buf = inet_frame_buf(main_idx)
        while 1 == 1
            val r = ipc_recv_notify(inet_port, buf, INET_MSG_BUF)
            inet_tcp_scan_timers()
            inet_udp_scan_timeouts()
            inet_ip_reasm_scan_timeouts()
            if r == -2
                notify_read_self()
                inet_drain_rx()
            else if r >= 0
                val sender = r
                val cmd = int(buf[0]) & 0xff
                cmd match
                    INET_CMD_SOCKET      -> inet_handle_socket(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_BIND        -> inet_handle_bind(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_SENDTO      -> inet_handle_sendto(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_RECVFROM    -> inet_handle_recvfrom(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_RECVFROM_TIMEOUT -> inet_handle_recvfrom_timeout(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_CLOSE       -> inet_handle_close(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_TCP_CONNECT -> inet_handle_tcp_connect(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_TCP_SEND    -> inet_handle_tcp_send(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_TCP_RECV    -> inet_handle_tcp_recv(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_TCP_CLOSE   -> inet_handle_tcp_close(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_TCP_SHUTDOWN -> inet_handle_tcp_shutdown(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_TCP_LISTEN  -> inet_handle_tcp_listen(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_TCP_ACCEPT  -> inet_handle_tcp_accept(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_SET_IP_CONFIG -> inet_handle_set_ip_config(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_GET_IP_CONFIG -> inet_handle_get_ip_config(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_ARP_PROBE   -> inet_handle_arp_probe(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_ICMP_SEND   -> inet_handle_icmp_send(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_ICMP_CHECK  -> inet_handle_icmp_check(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_PID_EXIT    -> inet_handle_pid_exit(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_UDP_GETNAME -> inet_handle_udp_getname(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_TCP_GETNAME -> inet_handle_tcp_getname(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_TCP_RECV_NB -> inet_handle_tcp_recv_nb(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_POLL        -> inet_handle_poll(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_EPOLL_SUB   -> inet_handle_epoll_sub(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_EPOLL_UNSUB -> inet_handle_epoll_unsub(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_EPOLL_INST_CLOSE -> inet_handle_epoll_inst_close(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_TCP_ACCEPT_NB -> inet_handle_tcp_accept_nb(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_TCP_CONNECT_NB -> inet_handle_tcp_connect_nb(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_TCP_SETBUF  -> inet_handle_tcp_setbuf(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_TCP_GETBUF  -> inet_handle_tcp_getbuf(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_TCP_ABORT_CLOSE -> inet_handle_tcp_abort_close(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_TCP_SET_KEEPALIVE -> inet_handle_tcp_set_keepalive(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_TCP_SET_KEEPOPT -> inet_handle_tcp_set_keepopt(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_TCP_GET_KEEPOPT -> inet_handle_tcp_get_keepopt(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_TCP_GET_ERR -> inet_handle_tcp_get_err(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_TCP_INJECT_FAIL -> inet_handle_tcp_inject_fail(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_TCP_GET_INFO -> inet_handle_tcp_get_info(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_TCP_DEBUG   -> inet_handle_tcp_debug(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_ICMP_UNREACH_INJECT -> inet_handle_icmp_unreach_inject(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_IP_REASM_SELFTEST -> inet_handle_ip_reasm_selftest(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_IP_REASM_OVERLAP_TEST -> inet_handle_ip_reasm_overlap_test(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_IP_REASM_TIMEOUT_TEST -> inet_handle_ip_reasm_timeout_test(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_TCP_SEND_PARK_TEST -> inet_handle_tcp_send_park_test(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_TCP_ARP_REPLAY_TEST -> inet_handle_tcp_arp_replay_test(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_PID_EXIT_SELFTEST -> inet_handle_pid_exit_selftest(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_GET_STATS         -> inet_handle_get_stats(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_FRAME_POOL_STATS  -> inet_handle_frame_pool_stats(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_TCP_OOO_SELFTEST -> inet_handle_tcp_ooo_selftest(sender, buf)
                    else ->
                        var reply: [1]byte
                        reply[0] = byte(-1)
                        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)
